[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How long should the stored userId persist? → sessionStorage (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m One more, then I'll lay out approaches.

  [1mThe fork:[0m "track who logged in" can mean two different deliverables, and they produce different code. Either the ID is
  [3mavailable[0m — held in an identity module other forms read — or it's [3mrecorded[0m — emitted somewhere you can go look at
  later. You've described the availability half ("other forms will need it"); I don't know if you also want the
  recording half now.

  - [1mAvailability only[0m — identity module holds the ID, exposes a getter, [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m backs it. Other forms read it
    when they're built. Smallest thing that satisfies what you've said. Nothing is written anywhere you can audit.
  - [1mAvailability + local event log[0m — the above, plus login events (who, when, success/failure) appended to a log the app
    can display or dump. Gives you something to actually look at, all client-side. Cost: another storage decision, and
    a client-side log is trivially editable, so it's a debugging aid, not an audit trail.
  - [1mAvailability + send events to a backend[0m — the above, plus POSTing login events to an endpoint. This is what a real
    audit trail needs. Cost: there's no backend here — [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m points at [38;5;153mapi.example.com[39m and the login body is a
    stub — so this is mostly design against an interface that doesn't exist yet.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m availability only, for this increment. It's the part you're certain you need, it unblocks the other
  forms, and the recording half is a clean addition on top once there's a real server to send to. Building a
  client-side log now risks it becoming the thing people mistake for an audit trail.
